/* The trait reference. bible 10.5: recognition is the counter system's
 * teaching surface, so this reads as a reference table, not a dialog.
 *
 * THE SHEET KEEPS ITS OWN SURFACE AND ITS OWN PADDING, unlike the three
 * screens landed beside it in Phase 8 Task 9, because it takes no
 * ScreenScaffold - `CodexSheet`'s class comment has the reason, and
 * `ScaffoldTests` exempts it by name. `.sheet-layer` in Shell.uss draws no
 * scrim, so this fill is the only thing between the sheet and the screen
 * under it: --paper would make the two the same colour.
 *
 * `.codex-entry` LOST ITS WHOLE RULE and gained `SectionCard`. Fill, radius,
 * padding, margin and type were all a re-derivation of what that component
 * already draws, and the elevation the card brings with it is what separates
 * a white card from this white sheet.
 *
 * ======================================================================
 * IT IS A BOTTOM SHEET AS OF PHASE 9 TASK 19, AND IT WAS DRAWN AT THE TOP
 * OF THE FRAME UNTIL THEN.
 *
 * Five specs call this one thing and they all call it the same thing:
 * `broodline_build_order.md:55` ("Build the Codex bottom sheet"),
 * `broodline_trait_codex.md:148` ("Tapping opens the entry as a bottom
 * sheet over whatever the player was doing"), `broodline_spec_reconciliation
 * .md:149`, `broodline_phase3_unity_client.md:25` and `client_architecture`
 * section 9, which `CodexSheet.cs` already quotes: "bottom sheets are an
 * overlay layer, not screens ... it overlays, it dismisses, it does not
 * push."
 *
 * What shipped was none of that. `.sheet-layer` is `position: absolute` with
 * no `justify-content`, so a child of it defaults to `flex-start` and this
 * sheet rendered flush with the TOP of the frame with paper below it -
 * visible in every capture of `CodexSheet.png` before this task, where the
 * sheet's white ends at y=640 of 932. Nothing asserted a position, so
 * nothing failed.
 *
 * THE SPLIT IS `AbandonedWaveSheet`'s, ELEMENT FOR ELEMENT, because that is
 * this sheet's sibling and the only other bottom sheet in the app: the ROOT
 * positions and nothing else, and `#surface` one level in carries the fill,
 * the radius and the padding. The radius is then TOP-ONLY, which is the
 * whole point of the arrangement - a sheet flush with the bottom edge has no
 * bottom corners to round, and rounding them anyway is what made the old
 * top-anchored sheet read as a floating card that had slid off the top.
 *
 * WHAT IS NOT COPIED FROM THE SIBLING, AND ON PURPOSE. `AbandonedWaveSheet`
 * draws a `--hud-scrim` and wraps its surface in `.elev-2`; this draws
 * neither. Both are composition this task was not asked for, and the
 * elevation one is the arrangement Theme.uss's header note 2 says is easy to
 * get wrong - `SectionCard.cs` records it shipping once already this phase.
 * Recorded as a residual rather than added quietly.
 * ====================================================================== */
.codex-sheet {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    /* No `flex-direction: column` here or on `#surface`: it is UI Toolkit's
     * default, `.abandoned-wave-sheet` omits it for that reason, and this
     * sheet's own standard is "does any declaration restate a component or a
     * Theme class". `justify-content: flex-end` therefore means the BOTTOM
     * edge, which is the whole arrangement. (`.codex-sheet__entries` below
     * still carries one; it predates this task and is left alone rather than
     * swept in, and it is named in the Task 19 report.) */
    justify-content: flex-end;
}

/* The paddings are the sibling's - 24 top and bottom, 16 each side
 * (`AbandonedWaveSheet.uss`). They are NOT the scaffold's 12px screen
 * gutter, and that difference is the sheet convention rather than a drift:
 * both sheets in this app inset their content further than a screen does. */
.codex-sheet__surface {
    background-color: var(--surface);
    border-top-left-radius: var(--radius-card);
    border-top-right-radius: var(--radius-card);
    padding-top: var(--space-6);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-6);
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    padding-right: var(--space-4);
}

.codex-sheet__title { margin-bottom: var(--space-3); }

.codex-sheet__entries { flex-direction: column; }

/* A sentence, not a label. Both lines of an entry's body wrap rather than
 * running off the sheet's right edge, which is what the old one-line row
 * did with "Found on Vetch Crawler." at 430px.
 *
 * `.codex-entry Label` REACHES THE CHIP'S LABEL TOO, and `white-space:
 * normal` is what a chip's label already has - Theme.uss sets no
 * `white-space` on `.chip` and UI Toolkit's default IS normal. So this
 * changes nothing about the chip; the chip does not wrap because it is
 * `flex-shrink: 0` and `align-self: flex-start`, which sizes it to its own
 * text. Checked rather than assumed, because a selector this broad is
 * exactly the shape that reaches further than its comment claims. */
.codex-entry Label { white-space: normal; }

/* THE ENTRY'S HEADING ROW - the trait's name on the left and the specimen
 * chip at the right edge, which is `.splice-chamber-view__card-header`'s
 * shape and the position a `GenChip` occupies on a roster card and a parent
 * tile. Declarations rather than a component, because a two-sided card
 * header is a screen's own furniture in this project: `SpliceChamberView`
 * and `FounderNamingView` each build one, and neither `SectionCard` nor
 * anything else owns it.
 *
 * `align-items: center` RATHER THAN `baseline`, which UI Toolkit does not
 * have at all - one of the four declarations this project records as
 * silently dropped. The chamber's own header note carries the same line.
 *
 * THE CHIP MOVED HERE IN FIX ROUND 1 AND IT WAS UNDER `found-on` BEFORE. A
 * chip's payload is its species tint, and `TraitCodexScreen.FoundOn` is
 * literally "Found on " + species - so stacked directly beneath that
 * sentence the chip repeated it on BOTH channels at once, in the worst
 * available position. Beside the heading it reads as a badge on the subject,
 * which is the swatch a reference table puts next to the term it defines and
 * the recognition surface bible 10.5 makes this sheet's job. The WORD is
 * still the heading's word; that is inherent to showing a specimen, and a
 * chip carrying anything else would not be the thing a player is learning to
 * recognise.
 *
 * THE `margin-bottom` IS `SectionCard`'s OWN, RE-STATED RATHER THAN
 * OVERRIDDEN. `.section-card__heading` puts --space-2 under a card's heading,
 * and this entry builds its own Label inside `Body` instead - so that rule
 * never reaches it and there is nothing here to cancel. Without this the row
 * sits flush against "Counters ...". Deliberately the same token, so a codex
 * entry and every other card in the app still break at the same distance
 * under their heading. */
.codex-entry__header {
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
}

/* The chip host, so the chip sits at the row's right edge rather than
 * stretching to the row's height - `.splice-chamber-view__gen`'s rule, for
 * the reason its own note gives.
 *
 * AND THERE IS NO GAP RULE HERE AT ALL, WHICH IS THE POINT OF THE ROW.
 * `TraitChip` already owns the handoff's `gap: 4px` as 2px of margin on each
 * of its own sides, so chips laid out horizontally need nothing added -
 * `.splice-chamber-view__parent-traits`' own note has the six pixels that
 * restating it cost there. A `margin-bottom` is what a COLUMN of chips
 * needs; the first draft of this rule carried one into a row, where it does
 * not space anything and pulls the single chip 2px off the row's centre
 * line. `.node__traits`' header records what each of the three positions in
 * the app actually spends. */
.codex-entry__traits { flex-direction: row; }

.codex-sheet__dismiss { margin-top: var(--space-3); }
